----+----+----+----+\n     a    b    c    d    e    f    g    h \nYour valid moves are (src-dst-promotion, or src-dst):\n[P: a7-a5, P: a7-a6, R: a8-b8, P: b7-b5, P: b7-b6, N: c6-a5, N: c6-b4, N: c6-b8, N: c6-d4, N: c6-e5, B: c8-d7, B: c8-e6, B: c8-f5, B: c8-g4, B: c8-h3, Q: d8-d7, Q: d8-d6, P: e7-e5, P: e7-e6, K: e8-d7, N: f6-d7, N: f6-e4, N: f6-g4, N: f6-g8, N: f6-h5, P: g7-g5, P: g7-g6, P: h7-h5, P: h7-h6, R: h8-g8]\nYour answer format should be: Answer: piece-name: move. \nPiece-name and move (src-dst-promotion or src-dst) should be directly copied EXACTLY from valid move list.\nExample of valid output format: N: a1-b3; P: e7-e8-q; Piece B: g5-f4.\nPlease select a move from your valid moves that maximize your probability of winning.\n', 'reasoning': '', 'output': 'Evaluation:  \nI am playing as Black, and it\'s my turn. The position shows that White has advanced the d4-pawn (PWF1), which is supported by its c3-knight and e2-pawn. My pawn on e7 is adjacent and can challenge central control. Several of my pieces are already developed: knights on c6 and f6, bishops on c8 and f8 (but f8 is actually empty — wait, correction: BBF0 is on f8? No — looking again: BBF0 is on f8? Wait, no: row 8 shows RBF0, _ , BBF0, QBF0, KBF0, BBF0, _ , RBF0 — so bishop on c8 and f8? Wait, c8 is BBF0? No: a8=RBF0, b8=empty, c8=BBF0? Wait, the board says:\n\nRow 8: a8=RBF0, b8=empty, c8=BBF0 → no, c8 is actually BBF0? But in the text: "c    d    e    f    g    h" and under c: BBF0? Let me re-parse.\n\nWait, the board:\n\na8: RBF0  \nb8: (empty)  \nc8: BBF0 ← this must be a typo in labeling? Because then d8: QBF0, e8: KBF0, f8: BBF0, g8: empty, h8: RBF0\n\nSo yes, Black has bishops on c8 and f8, queen on d8, king on e8, rooks on a8 and h8. All good.\n\nNow, key observations:\n\n- White played P: d2-d4 earlier? Not exactly visible, but there\'s a PWF1 on d4, meaning it moved one step and has moved before — so likely d2-d4 was played two moves ago? But now, importantly, there is a white pawn on d4 and a black pawn on e7. Also, a white pawn on d4 and black pawns on e7 and c7.\n\nBut more importantly: there is a white pawn on d4 and a black pawn on e7. However, notice that on d5 there is a black pawn: PBF1 on d5? Wait no — look:\n\nRow 5: d5 = PBF1? No — row 5: "d" column is PBF1? Wait:\n\nRow 5: `|    |    |    |PBF1|    |    |    |    |` → so d5 has PBF1? That’s a black pawn on d5.\n\nWait — that changes everything.\n\nLet me recheck:\n\n- d5: PBF1 → Black pawn on d5\n- d4: PWF1 → White pawn on d4\n\nThey are adjacent! So they’re locked. So no immediate e5 push threatens d4.\n\nBut now, what about e7-e5? That’s in the valid move list.\n\nWait — e7-e5 would put a second black pawn on e5, but d5 already has a pawn. Is that possible?\n\nNo — e7-e5 would move the e7 pawn to e5, leaving d5 still occupied. That’s fine — two pawns on d5 and e5 would create a strong center.\n\nBut let\'s see if e7-e5 is legal.\n\nCurrently, e5 is empty — nothing on e5. So yes, e7-e5 is a legal pawn move.\n\nMoreover, it gains space, fights for the center, activates the f8 bishop (which is currently blocked), and prepares kingside development.\n\nOther options:\n\n- Nf6-e4: knight to e4 — attacks d2, f2, could be strong, especially since White’s king is still in the center? But White has castled? Wait — KWF0 — king never moved, so likely still in center. But look at rook on h1 and a1 — both RWF0 — never moved. So White hasn’t castled. King is still on e1.\n\nSo Ne4 would attack f2, which is only defended by the king — tempting.\n\nBut after Nf6-e4, White can play f3 or Nxe4, but if Nxe4, then dxe4, opening the d-file.\n\nAlternatively, e7-e5 immediately supports d5 and opens the bishop.\n\nAnother idea: d5-d4? But not listed — so probably blocked? d4 has a white pawn — so d5-d4 is capture? But not in move list — so maybe not allowed because it would be d5xd4, but perhaps it\'s not considered in the valid moves provided.\n\nValid moves include: P: e7-e5, which is promising.\n\nAlso, P: b7-b5 — flank pawn move, maybe preparing queenside expansion, but less urgent.\n\nN: c6-e5 — not listed? Wait, N: c6-a5, b4, b8, d4, e5 — yes! N: c6-e5 **is** listed.\n\nNc6-e5 would jump to e5, attacking f7 and d7? Wait — no, from e5, it attacks f7 (white king!), d7, c6, etc. But f7 is defended only by the king? Yes — f7 has PWF0 — so only king defends it. So Nxe5? But wait — e5 is empty? Currently, e5 is empty. So Nc6-e5 is possible.\n\nBut White can respond with f3 or Nxe5, and if Nxe5, dxe5, then we have doubled pawns but central control.\n\nBut better might be to play e7-e5 first, supporting d5 and freeing the f8 bishop.\n\nWait — but where is the f8 bishop? Row 8: f8 has BBF0 — yes, bishop on f8.\n\nCurrently blocked by e7 pawn. So e7-e5 opens the diagonal for the bishop.\n\nSimilarly, d5-d4 would open the c8 bishop, but d4 is occupied.\n\nBut e7-e5 seems very thematic.\n\nAnother candidate: Qd8-d6 — bringing queen to d6, eyeing h2 or supporting e5.\n\nBut possibly premature — White may attack with Ng5 or something.\n\nBack to e7-e5: it’s solid, principled, improves piece activity, and asserts central dominance.\n\nNe4 is also aggressive: Nf6-e4.\n\nFrom e4, the knight is very strong — forks f2 and d2, and is hard to dislodge.\n\nAfter Ne4, if White plays f3, then Nc5 or Ng5; if Nxe4, then dxe4, opening the d-file for the queen or rook.\n\nBut capturing on e4 gives White an isolated d-pawn? After dxe4, d4 becomes isolated if c3 knight recaptures? Not necessarily — c3 knight can take back.\n\nWhite has a knight on c3 (NWF1).\n\nSo if Ne4, and Nxe4, dxe4, then White has d4 pawn isolated? No — because c3 knight can recapture: dxe4, Nc3xe4 — then no problem.\n\nSo Ne4 might just be exchanged.\n\nBut e7-e5 avoids early simplification and builds up.\n\nAlso, note that d5 and e5 pawns form a strong duo.\n\nMoreover, after e7-e5, the next move could be Nb8-c6 (but already done), or Bf8-e7, 0-0, etc.\n\nAnother point: White has a pawn on d4, which is somewhat overextended.\n\nBy playing e7-e5, we fix the d4 pawn as a target later.\n\nAlso, our knight on f6 can go to e4 or g4.\n\nBut let’s compare:\n\n- P: e7-e5 — strengthens center, frees bishop, prepares kingside castling.\n- N: f6-e4 — immediate tactical shot, but may be met accurately.\n\nGiven that the position is still developing, I prefer the strategic buildup.\n\nQd8-d6 is playable but blocks the d-file and may get harassed.\n\nRook moves like a8-b8 are slow.\n\nTherefore, e7-e5 stands out.\n\nIs en passant possible? After e7-e5, the pawn moves two squares, so it becomes en passant eligible — but that’s fine unless White has a d4 pawn — which they do — so White could play d4xe6 en passant if we play e7-e5.\n\nBut that would open the e-file and trade a central pawn — but we’d recapture with fxe6 or dxe6? We have d5 pawn — so after d4xe6, we can recapture with d5xe6 — maintaining central presence.\n\nSo even if en passant is taken, we’re fine.\n\nThus, e7-e5 is safe.\n\nReason:  \nPlaying e7-e5 strengthens central control, supports the d5 pawn, opens the diagonal for the f8 bishop, and prepares kingside development including potential castling. It’s a flexible and principled move that increases pressure without overcommitting tactically.\n\nAnswer: P: e7-e5'}
